<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<!--
  What a macOS build outside the Mac App Store applies to the Share Extension: the dev loop, CI,
  and the notarized Developer ID .dmg. The Store archive swaps in AllodiaMailShare.entitlements
  instead (MACOS_SHARE_ENTITLEMENTS in project.yml), which is the App Group set.

  ⚠️ The App Group is deliberately absent here, and this is a measurement rather than a
  preference (2026-09-20, macOS 27):

    * A macOS target whose entitlements name com.apple.security.application-groups is refused by
      the build system outright, "AllodiaMailShare requires a provisioning profile", whatever
      CODE_SIGN_IDENTITY says. PROVISIONING_PROFILE_REQUIRED=NO does not suppress it.
    * Applying it with `codesign` afterwards gets past the build and fails at runtime instead: the
      signature carries the group, `containerURL` answers with a path, and every write to it
      returns EPERM. Measured with both `group.<id>` and `<team>.group.<id>`, on a directory that
      had never been touched, so it is neither the prefix nor a poisoned container. A sandboxed
      process simply cannot use an App Group a provisioning profile did not grant.

  A build here has no profile, and neither CI nor a checkout without an Apple account can get one,
  so the box is a directory under the user's home instead, and this grant is what reaches it. The
  app is not sandboxed in these builds and reads that path with no entitlement at all.

  The path is the whole grant, so it is as narrow as the box: not the engine store, not the
  credential index, not the diagnostic log. It must stay in step with
  `ShareBox.homeRelativePath`.
-->
<dict>
    <!-- macOS will not load an app extension that is not sandboxed, and the Store rejects any
         executable in a submission that is not (ITMS-90296). -->
    <key>com.apple.security.app-sandbox</key>
    <true/>

    <!-- The drop box, and nothing else under the user's home. Leading and trailing slash are both
         load-bearing: the path is home-relative, and the trailing one makes it a directory grant
         rather than a single file. -->
    <key>com.apple.security.temporary-exception.files.home-relative-path.read-write</key>
    <array>
        <string>/.local/share/mailcal-share/</string>
    </array>
</dict>
</plist>
